WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.744
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 27.

2
00:00:05.364 --> 00:00:10.533
Question one reads three characters one at a time, prints input is equal to x for each

3
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:15.587
one that equals the character x, then prints the characters in reverse order.

4
00:00:15.587 --> 00:00:18.746
For the input q, x, z, what appears?

5
00:00:20.446 --> 00:00:24.552
Compare each char with the character literal x, in single quotes.

6
00:00:24.552 --> 00:00:28.279
Only the second input matches, so the message prints once.

7
00:00:28.899 --> 00:00:33.436
Then print in reverse: c three, c two, c one. That is z, x, q.

8
00:00:35.036 --> 00:00:38.605
The theory: single quotes make a char, one character.

9
00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:40.441
Double quotes make a string.

10
00:00:40.441 --> 00:00:43.170
And without quotes, x is a variable name.

11
00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.319
Only the char literal compares correctly with a char.

12
00:00:47.919 --> 00:00:50.776
So the message prints once, then z, x, q.

13
00:00:51.595 --> 00:00:54.355
Writing c equals equals x in double quotes, or

14
00:00:54.355 --> 00:00:57.392
with no quotes, instead of the char literal.

15
00:00:58.113 --> 00:01:02.807
Exam tip: to reverse a few separate variables, just print them last to first.

16
00:01:02.807 --> 00:01:04.083
No loop is needed.
